{
  "bioavailability_prediction": "low",
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "revision_action": "keep",
  "supportive_card_ids": ["C32", "C56", "C51", "C58"],
  "contradictory_card_ids": ["C03"],
  "prediction_basis_card_ids": ["C32", "C56", "C51", "C58"],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Exp3174, an imidazole/butyl/N-benzyl acidic analog of the query, shows very low Caco-2 absorptive permeability (A-B Papp 2.50e-8 cm/s) and high efflux (B-A/A-B ~10; ER 3.5 with BCRP inhibition), supporting poor intestinal absorption for the query's dianionic dicarboxylate scaffold.",
      "card_ids": ["C56", "C51", "C58"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Azosemide, a directly measured low-F analog, has an L2 absolute F estimate of about 10% in fasted normal subjects based on urinary recovery (2% oral vs 20% IV unchanged drug), affirmatively below the F>=20% threshold; an L1 estimate of ~20.4% conflicts, so this analog evidence is low-transferability and not decisive alone.",
      "card_ids": ["C32", "C03"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The query's physicochemical prior (two ionized carboxylic acids at intestinal pH, logD 0.56, TPSA 92.4 Å², 10 rotatable bonds) is consistent with poor passive absorption and provides no affirmative evidence for F>=20%.",
      "card_ids": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall efflux/intestinal metabolism records provide no direct query measurement; C63 shows very poor Caco-2 permeability for a charged molecule (otilonium), but transfer to the query is low, and no L5 record overturns the low prediction.",
      "card_ids": ["C63"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C63", "C69", "C71"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability, AUC, or Cmax measurement for the query molecule is present in the visible records.",
    "L5 gut-wall efflux/intestinal metabolism records are for other molecules (imatinib, losartan, TM-25659, otilonium, repaglinide, etc.), not the query; no direct intestinal metabolism data are available.",
    "Azosemide estimates conflict (C03 ~20.4% vs C32 ~10%) and azosemide differs from the query in key ionizable groups.",
    "Exp3174 is a metabolite rather than an orally administered drug; its low permeability may not fully represent the query's absorption.",
    "No measured first-pass/hepatic extraction or formulation data for the query; low F could also be influenced by metabolism/clearance.",
    "No direct evidence on whether the query is a substrate of intestinal efflux transporters."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L5 gut-wall efflux/intestinal metabolism evidence is not directly applicable to the query and does not overturn the prior low prediction; combined with Exp3174 low-permeability/high-efflux data, the conflicting but affirmative low azosemide estimate, and the unfavorable dianionic physicochemical prior, F<20% remains better supported, so the prediction is low with moderate confidence."
}

